# ── Build-process litter ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every one of these has been committed to this repo at least once, usually by
# uploading a file through the GitHub web UI — which does NOT honour .gitignore.
# Keeping the rules anyway so local `git add -A` stays safe.
*.patch
*.diff
apply-*.sh
*.zip
.backups/

# The npm lockfile is not used here — pnpm-lock.yaml is authoritative.
package-lock.json

# Patch files are a transport format, not source.
#
# FOUR of them were committed to the repository root: 0082truthcontrolprobe.patch,
# 0001searchai20260824.patch, ecowoodsseov2.patch and ecowoods_seo_v3.patch. The last two are this
# branch's own patches, uploaded through the GitHub web UI — which silently
# strips the dashes from a filename, which is why `git am 0001-search-ai-...`
# and `git am ecowoods-seo-v2.patch` both failed with "No such file or
# directory" while the file sat in the directory the command was run from.
#
# If you are here because a patch will not apply: `ls *.patch` first. The name
# on disk is not the name it was sent under.
#
# A committed patch is a second, frozen copy of changes that also exist in the
# history, and it will drift from them the moment anything is amended. It is
# also 500 KB of diff text that every clone, every CI checkout and every crawler
# of the raw repository now carries forever. Deliver a patch as a file; apply it;
# delete it.
*.patch
